  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\u22481.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\u22480.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061e=e-2\u22480.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)",
